PR #264 - changes required
1. [BLOCKER] `backend/openbot/runtime/prompt.py:15,33` and `backend/openbot/runtime/system_prompt.md:33` - this still changes the shared prompt placeholder contract by adding `repository_instructions` to the strict allowlist and template, then filling it via `Template.substitute` at line 143. The PR description says repository instructions are appended after normal template substitution and that the supported placeholder set remains unchanged, but the actual code does the opposite. Any consumer or startup check that relies on the existing shared-template contract will now require this new placeholder. Render the validated existing template first, then append the repository-instructions section to the resulting prompt, and keep `_SYSTEM_PROMPT_FIELDS`/`system_prompt.md` unchanged; add a regression that proves the original placeholder set remains accepted.
2. [BLOCKER] `backend/openbot/runtime/prompt.py:49-60` - discovery walks from the thread workspace root all the way to the filesystem root instead of stopping at the configured workspace root. If a workspace is `/srv/openbot/workspace/repo` and `/srv/openbot/workspace/AGENTS.md` exists, a bot working in `repo` receives that parent file even though it is outside the repository/workspace boundary; this can inject unrelated or sensitive instructions. Stop after checking the intended workspace root (while preserving the nearest-directory precedence), and test that a parent above the workspace is not loaded.
3. [BLOCKER] `backend/openbot/api/schemas.py:490-499` / `backend/openbot/api/bots.py:96-108` - the catalog API does not expose `load_repository_instructions`. `BotCatalogEntry` contains the new field and installation consumes it, but `BotCatalogEntryOut` omits it, so `/bots/catalog` silently drops the setting and clients cannot observe the catalog contract. Add the field to the response schema and include an API regression covering the engineer catalog entry and the default false value for other entries.
4. [BLOCKER] `backend/tests/test_prompt.py:185-225` - the new behavior is only tested at the prompt helper level. There is no regression coverage for the changed BotCreate/BotUpdate/BotOut API fields, catalog listing/installation, seed synchronization, migration-created defaults, or the builtin `create_bot` path. These are production behavior changes and the mandatory failure/contract coverage is missing; add tests that would fail if each integration were reverted.

Exact-head verification: reviewed `42fc47c5891858cd4070bec04e79fb5bd7ecaefc`; the current backend and frontend check runs both report success for that SHA; no inline, conversation, or formal review comments were returned. Mergeability is reported as clean, but the blockers above remain.

